RAG Agent
An AI-powered Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) assistant that can answer questions using your uploaded documents, search the web when needed, and perform calculations.

Overview
This project combines document retrieval with an LLM-based agent. It is designed to provide answers grounded in local knowledge sources while still being able to use external search for questions that are not covered by the documents.

Key capabilities
Document Q&A: Retrieves relevant chunks from documents stored in the data/ directory.
Agentic workflow: Uses a LangGraph ReAct agent to decide which tool to call.
Web search: Uses Tavily Search when document retrieval does not provide the needed information.
Calculator: Evaluates mathematical expressions through an agent tool.
Two ways to interact: Includes a Streamlit application and a command-line entry point.
Tech stack
Python
LangChain
LangGraph
Groq-hosted language model (openai/gpt-oss-20b, as configured in the agent)
Streamlit
Tavily Search
FAISS for vector similarity search
Sentence Transformers (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) for local text embeddings
PyMuPDF for PDF text extraction
Exact package versions are maintained in requirements.txt. Check that file if you are setting up a fresh environment.

Project structure
RAG-AGENT/
├── data/                 # Put source documents here
├── .env.example          # Environment-variable template
├── .gitignore
├── agent.py              # Agentic RAG workflow and tools
├── app.py                # Streamlit user interface
├── main.py               # Command-line entry point
├── rag_engine.py         # Document loading, indexing and retrieval
└── requirements.txt      # Python dependencies
How it works
Load documents: Files placed in data/ are loaded by the RAG engine.
Split into chunks: The content is divided into smaller text chunks suitable for retrieval.
Create embeddings and index: Text chunks are converted into embeddings and stored in a searchable vector index.
Retrieve context: When a question is asked, the retriever finds the most relevant chunks.
Generate an answer: The language model uses the retrieved context to formulate a response.
Use additional tools when needed: The agent can call web search or the calculator tool depending on the question and its instructions.
Getting started
1. Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/KRSNA444/RAG-AGENT.git
cd RAG-AGENT
2. Create and activate a virtual environment
Windows PowerShell

python -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
macOS / Linux

python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
3. Install dependencies
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
pip install -r requirements.txt
4. Configure environment variables
Copy .env.example to .env and add the API keys required by the features you use. The project uses Groq for the LLM and Tavily for web search.

Windows PowerShell

Copy-Item .env.example .env
macOS / Linux

cp .env.example .env
Open .env and fill in the variable names exactly as shown in .env.example.

Typical credentials required by this project:

GROQ_API_KEY=your_groq_api_key
TAVILY_API_KEY=your_tavily_api_key
If .env.example defines different names, follow the template in the repository. Never commit your real .env file or expose API keys publicly.

5. Add your documents
Place supported source documents in the data/ directory. The retrieval pipeline currently reads .pdf files from data/ using PyMuPDF. It splits extracted text into 500-character chunks with 50-character overlap, embeds them with all-MiniLM-L6-v2, and stores the vectors in a FAISS index.

6. Run the application
Streamlit interface

streamlit run app.py
Command-line interface

python main.py
The repository also contains agent.py, which defines the agentic workflow and its tools. Run it directly only if you want to use its interactive terminal loop:

python agent.py
Environment variables
Variable	Purpose
GROQ_API_KEY	Authenticates requests to the Groq-hosted language model
TAVILY_API_KEY	Authenticates Tavily web-search requests used by the agent workflow
Use the exact names present in .env.example if they differ from the examples above.

Main components
rag_engine.py
Responsible for loading PDFs with PyMuPDF, splitting extracted text into chunks, generating Sentence Transformers embeddings, building a FAISS index and retrieving relevant chunks.

agent.py
Defines the agentic workflow. It configures the LLM and exposes tools for document search, web search and calculations. The document-search tool is instructed to run first for questions about the supported web-development topics.

app.py
Contains the Streamlit interface for interacting with the assistant.

main.py
Provides a command-line way to use the RAG assistant.

Troubleshooting
Missing API key: Check that .env exists in the project root and contains valid credentials.
Module not found: Activate the virtual environment and run pip install -r requirements.txt.
No document results: Confirm that files are in data/, are supported by the loader, and have been indexed.
Web search errors: Verify the Tavily API key and account limits.
Groq/model errors: Verify the Groq API key and confirm that the configured model is available to your account.
PowerShell activation blocked: You may need to adjust the execution policy for the current PowerShell session, or use the virtual environment's Python executable directly.
Security
Keep .env out of Git.
Share .env.example with placeholder values only.
Do not upload private documents unless you are comfortable with the configured embedding, LLM and search services processing relevant content.
Treat retrieved document text and web results as untrusted input.
Future improvements
Add support for more document formats.
Display source citations and retrieved passages in the UI.
Add chat history and conversation persistence.
Add document management (upload, remove and re-index).
Improve error handling, logging and automated tests.
Add deployment instructions and configuration validation.
